// Real thermodynamic data. dHf is standard enthalpy of formation in kJ/mol,
// S is standard molar entropy in J/(mol.K), Cp is molar heat capacity in
// J/(mol.K), melt and boil are in kelvin, density in g/cm3.
//
// EVERY reaction below is checked for balance when this file loads. An equation
// that does not conserve atoms and charge will REFUSE TO LOAD and name the
// offending element -- there is no silent creation of matter here. Nuclear
// reactions are the one exception, and correctly so: they transmute elements,
// and what they conserve is nucleon number and charge, which is what is checked
// for them instead.
//
// Ea is the activation energy in kJ/mol and A the Arrhenius pre-exponential
// factor; together they decide whether a thermodynamically downhill reaction is
// actually fast enough to happen at the temperature available. That is what
// stops iron oxide reducing at room temperature just because the free energy
// permits it.
